course: politics
lesson: 42
title: Olympiad: check the claim and spot the trick ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In this olympiad lesson every puzzle hides a trick: a number that sounds huge, a clip cut short, a crowd cropped, a question pretending to be a fact; your job is to find it before you believe or share.
explain: Numbers can shout: "bike thefts up 100%!" sounds like a crime wave, but in a village it may mean 1 theft last year and 2 this year, just one more bike.
explain: Who was asked matters as much as the answer: "9 out of 10 kids love the new lunch" means little if the 10 kids were all standing in the pizza queue.
explain: Clips, crops and captions can bend the truth even when every word or picture is real, so look for the full speech, the wide photo and the date before you decide.
explain: Real people pile on hyperbole and play things down: "a million people at the rally" in a square that holds 5,000 is an exaggeration to spot, and "not sure yet" is always an honest answer.
fact: A percentage change can sound huge when the real numbers are tiny: from 1 to 2 is "up 100%", from 2 to 3 is "up 50%", and from 4 to 6 is also "up 50%".
fact: A survey only tells you about the people it asked; asking just one kind of person, like the pizza queue, gives a lopsided answer.
fact: Cherry-picking means choosing only the bits that fit; cutting a clip so its meaning changes is taking it out of context, even if every word is real.
fact: Cropping a photo can make a crowd look huge or tiny, and old videos are sometimes shared with new captions as if they were happening now; this is recycled footage.
fact: A headline asking a scary question, like "Will the council ban football?", is not a fact, and headlines often leave out the "maybe" and "only" in the story underneath.
fact: Propaganda often uses fear, anger, music and colours instead of reasons, may call a whole group "enemies", and shows only the facts that help one side.
fact: Loaded words, such as "brave heroes" or "an angry mob", tell you how to feel; plain words, such as "about 200 people stood outside the town hall", tell you what happened.
fact: Misinformation is false information shared by mistake; disinformation is false information spread on purpose; a mistake fixed openly with a correction is not a lie.
fact: Likes, shares and a copied logo do not make a story true; a screenshot is easy to change, so find the real article on the outlet's own website.
fact: To check a claim, ask who says it, how they know, and who else reports it; if you cannot find out yet, "not sure yet" is an honest answer.
fact: Satire is a joke that copies the style of news, like The Onion in the USA or The Beaverton in Canada.
fact: The King and the royal family do not vote and do not say which party they support, so a post saying the King voted for a party is false.
fact: Yard signs show what some households think, not who will win, because most people never put one up; calling a true story "fake news" because you dislike it does not make it false.
fact: Words in this lesson: "it's giving" means it looks like; "bare" in London means lots of; "dreich" in Scotland means grey and wet; "craic" in Ireland means fun or news; "can or not" in Singapore English means is it OK.
exercise: A village newsletter shouts "Bike thefts up 100%!" Last year 1 bike was stolen. How many this year? | 2 | 100 | 101 | 200
exercise: "Litter fines up 50%!" Last month there were 4 fines. How many this month? | 6 | 54 | 8 | 50
exercise: An advert says "9 out of 10 kids love our new lunch." The 10 kids were all in the pizza queue. What is the trick? | They only asked kids who had already chosen pizza | Ten kids is far too many for a fair survey | The kids in the queue were actors, not real | Nine out of ten is a number that is always made up
exercise: The mayor said, "Some want to close the library. I say no." A clip shows only "close the library". What is the trick? | Taking the mayor's words out of context | A deepfake that a computer made up from nothing | Satire, a joke that copies the news | Recycled footage from an old year
exercise: Two photos of one rally: a tight crop shows a packed crowd, a wide shot shows a half-empty square. Which helps you judge the crowd best? | The wide shot, which shows the whole square | The crop, because it is closer to the people | Both equally, because photos never lie | Neither, so the crowd must have been huge
exercise: A video captioned "snowstorm in London TODAY" is posted on a hot July day. A news site shows the same video from 2018. What is it? | Recycled footage with a new caption | Live news filmed this very morning | Satire from a joke news website | A real snowstorm that came in July
exercise: Headline: "Will the council ban football in parks?" The story says one councillor asked about mud on one pitch. What is true? | Nothing is banned; the headline is a question | Football is now banned in every park in town | The council has voted to ban football | One councillor banned football all by himself
exercise: Odd one out: scary music; calling a whole group "enemies"; a dated link to the full report; only good news about the leader. Which is odd, and why? | The link, as it helps you check | The music, as news never has music in it | The enemies, as only wars use that word | The good news, as good news is always true
exercise: Gran shares a fake story believing it is true. Its maker invented it on purpose. What are these? | Gran: misinformation; the maker: disinformation | Gran: disinformation; the maker: misinformation | Both of them are misinformation | Both of them are disinformation
exercise: Which description uses plain words, not loaded ones? | About 200 people stood outside the town hall | A brave crowd of heroes stood up for our town | An angry mob swarmed all over the town hall | Troublemakers wasted everyone's time again
exercise: A post says "The King voted for Party A!" and has 50,000 likes. What is the best verdict? | False: the King does not vote, and likes prove nothing | True, because 50,000 people have liked it | Not sure yet, until all of the likes on the post have been counted | True, since the King must vote in every election
exercise: Put the checking questions in the course's order. | Who says it, how do they know, who else reports it | Who else reports it, who says it, how many likes it has got so far | How many likes, who says it, does it sound scary | Is it shouty, is it funny, who shared it with me
exercise: Headline: "PM says school holidays will be cut!" Article: "The PM said she might look at cutting one day, maybe in 2030." What did the headline drop? | "might" and "one day" | "PM" and "school" | "holidays" and "cut" | "says" and "will"
exercise: Every house on Mia's street has a sign for Jones. Mia says Jones will win the whole town. What is wrong? | Most people never put up a sign, so signs show only a few homes | Yard signs are counted as real votes on election day | Jones put all the signs up himself, so none count | Nothing; one street always decides a whole town
exercise: The Beaverton runs the headline "Moose elected mayor of Toronto." What should you decide? | It is satire, a joke written like news | It is real news, as it looks like news | It is disinformation spread by moose | It is propaganda paid for by the mayor
test: "BARE people at the rally, like a MILLION, I swear," says Leo. The square holds about 5,000. ("bare" means lots of.) What really happened? | Lots came, but no more than about 5,000 | Exactly one million came, as he counted them | Five million came, as the square was stretched | Nobody came, since he was exaggerating
test: "it's giving fake: sus logo, no date, no author," says Priya about a post. ("it's giving" means it looks like.) What is the fair conclusion? | Several warning clues together: check before you share | One clue alone proves it fake, so laugh at whoever posted it | Logos cannot be copied, so the post must be real | No date means the post was written today
test: Uncle Seamus: "Ah, the craic on the group chat is the mayor's banning dogs." ("craic" here means news.) Nothing on the council site or any news site. Best reply? | Probably false, as nothing official has it | True, because the group chat said so and it is busy | False, because group chats always lie about everything | True, because councils ban dogs most weeks
test: Dad, on a dreich Glasgow day: "Poll says most people hate the new park! They asked 20 folk at the car-park protest." ("dreich" means grey and wet.) What's wrong with the poll? | It asked only people who were already protesting | 20 people is far too many to ask in one poll | Polls taken on rainy days never count at all | Car parks are not allowed in polls by law
test: Singapore classmate: "Clip got minister saying 'raise taxes', 5 seconds only. Share already, can or not?" ("can or not" means is it OK.) What do you say? | Not yet; find the full speech, as words like "not" may be cut | Yes, a clip that short cannot be changed | Yes, ministers always mean what clips show | No, ministers never talk about taxes
test: Little brother: "Teacher said lost lunchboxes are up 200%!!! we're doomed." It went from 1 lost lunchbox to 3. How bad is it really? | Two more lost lunchboxes | 200 more lunchboxes lost this term | The school is really, truly doomed | Three hundred lunchboxes missing now
test: A poster shows dark clouds and says "THEY are coming to ruin everything. Vote for us!" Which tricks does it use? | Fear, and calling a group the enemy instead of giving reasons | Plain facts with dates and links to check, so you can see it is fair | Satire, so it is just a harmless joke | A careful poll with a margin of error
test: Your mate: "Fake news! That story about our school winning a prize is fake, coz I hate our school lol." The story is on the council website. What is true? | Calling a true story fake doesn't make it fake | It's fake, because he said "fake news" first | It's fake, because council websites can't be trusted | It's only true once 100 people like it
test: Gran: "There's been a teeny mix-up in the paper, love." It printed the wrong winner of the town vote, then a correction. What does the correction show? | The paper made a mistake and fixed it openly | The paper told a lie on purpose to fool people | The winner changed after the paper was printed | Corrections prove the paper is fake news
test: In Atlanta: "Y'all, a screenshot says the news says school's cancelled all week!" The news site's own page says nothing. Which counts? | The news site's own page | The screenshot, because it has the logo | Whichever one has more shares | Both, so school is half cancelled
test: A meme shows a president's face and the words "Children should work in mines", with no date and no link. Best move? | Look for it in a report, video or speech first | Believe it, because the face is real | Share it with the words "can't believe this" | Decide it's true if it makes you angry
test: One report says "brave heroes defend our town", another says "angry mob blocks roads", about the same march. What can both probably agree on? | People marched in the town that day | The marchers were all heroes | The marchers were all a mob | Nobody marched at all that day
exam: It is given that the number of reported incidents rose from two to three. Determine the percentage increase. | Fifty per cent | One hundred per cent | One per cent | Thirty per cent
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of a survey conducted exclusively among persons already queuing for the product in question. | The persons consulted do not represent all | The number of persons consulted is excessively large | Surveys may not lawfully be conducted out of doors | Persons in a queue are incapable of answering
exam: Select the description most consistent with the practice of cherry-picking. | The selection solely of the portions that support one view | The examination of every portion of the evidence | The correction of an error in public | The consultation of several independent outlets
exam: Whereas a statement can be neither confirmed nor refuted at present, determine the most honest verdict. | Not sure yet | True | False | True, provided many have shared it
